AB  - Visual object tracking (VOT) is vital in pattern recognition. Traditional methods often use classification and regression heads. Recent autoregressive methods in visual tracking, treating tracking as bounding-box sequence generation, have shown better performance by framing tracking as discrete sequence generation. These approaches convert bounding box coordinates to discrete tokens in a shared vocabulary for sequential regression. However, this shift from a continuous to a discrete representation introduces quantization and positional biases, adversely affecting performance with larger vocabularies. To overcome this constraint, we propose a diffusion-based sequence learning tracking approach, named DSTrack. In this method, we employ the diffusion model to predict bounding-box coordinates in a parallel manner and transform the discrete variable distribution estimation into continuous probability distribution estimation. Furthermore, to enhance the representational prowess of the Vision Transformer (ViT) backbones, we amplify the pre-trained ViT model by integrating additional Ladder modules. This strategic enhancement is designed to effectively bridge the gaps between different dataset domains. This novel diffusion-based sequence learning paradigm theoretically circumvents the constraints of the discrete token vocabulary and improves tracking performance. With fair comparison in our experiments, the proposed DSTrack achieves state-of-the-art results on benchmark datasets such as LaSOT and TrackingNet.
